<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard Wagner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard Wagner was a German composer and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> director whose operas, which he called music dramas, transformed European music. His works include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tristan-und-isolde">Tristan und Isolde</a>, Die Meistersinger von Nürnberg, the four-part Ring cycle and Parsifal. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-ii-of-bavaria">Ludwig II of Bavaria</a> became his patron in 1864, paid his debts and helped finance the festival theatre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a>, opened in 1876. His antisemitic writings have overshadowed his legacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-wagner.jpg" alt="Richard Wagner" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Hanfstaengl · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RichardWagner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ludwig II made Munich the scene of <b>Richard Wagner</b>'s Tristan und Isolde in 1865 and helped finance the festival theatre at Bayreuth, and his castles of Neuschwanstein, Linderhof and Herrenchiemsee became some of the most visited sights in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Richard Wagner</b> drew on the Eddas and related sagas for his opera cycle The Ring of the Nibelung, and J.R.R. Tolkien, a scholar of Old Norse, took the names of his dwarves and of Gandalf from the Völuspá.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Nineteenth-century Romantics and nationalists made them heroic ancestors, and the horned helmets of popular imagination come from that age, notably from the costumes for <b>Richard Wagner</b>’s Ring cycle at Bayreuth in 1876; the only nearly complete Viking helmet, found at Gjermundbu in Norway in 1943, has none.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In music the Romantic style lived on through <b>Richard Wagner</b>, Johannes Brahms and Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky to the end of the century.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Richard Wagner</b> later built his festival theatre in the town.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-brandenburg-bayreuth">Principality of Brandenburg-Bayreuth</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.34,12.38&amp;year=1858" aria-label="Leipzig on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4830 385 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Richard Wagner"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5280 655h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5280 655h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.34,12.38&amp;year=1858">What was here in 1858? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
